INT. PHOTO BOOTH, WEDDING RECEPTION - NIGHT

A curtained booth, red velvet, a strip of lightbulbs. Muffled dance music thumps through the walls.

INGRID SOLBERG, thirty, in a bridesmaid's green dress, sits on the stool with her shoes in her lap. THEO MARCHETTI, thirty-two, a groomsman, slides in beside her, tie loosened. PASCAL, the photographer's assistant, nineteen, sticks his head through the curtain with a coin tray.

PASCAL
Four frames. Strike a pose.

INGRID
We're not together.

THEO
We're not together.

INGRID ^
Do not make this weird.

The flash goes. Ingrid and Theo both flinch and nearly fall off the stool. Pascal ducks out.

THEO
We haven't spoken in three years.

INGRID
We spoke in March.

THEO
You said "excuse me."

INGRID
You were in the way of the cheese.

The flash goes again. Neither smiles.

THEO
You look good.

INGRID
Don't.

THEO
I'm just saying.

INGRID
I know what you're saying. You're saying it with that face.

Flash. She is looking at him. He is looking at her. Flash. She leans in and kisses him, hard, and the booth shakes. The curtain parts.

PASCAL
(whispering)
Do you want me to keep the strip?
